  "live_records": {
    "q-124659": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124659",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,3)$ to the point $N(3,-1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(3\\,i-j-(i+3\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3-4t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=3-4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+1-(3-4t)}(2+(-4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6t-2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{6(1)-2}-{e}^{6(0)-2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{6}}{3{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
