    "q-124662": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124662",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-(i+j)) \\\\  & =i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{(2t+1)^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-\\frac{1}{2t+1}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+1}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0+1}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-124662-a",
