    "q-80983": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80983",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
