    "q-81178": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81178",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
