    "q-124666": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124666",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $P(1,0)$ to point $Q(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+2{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+2]-[\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}+2-\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
