    "q-90848": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90848",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
